          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win all 52 cards by winning battles and wars. Higher card wins each battle (Ace high). On tie, escalation war: number of face-down cards depends on the rank that caused the tie (e.g. 7 to 7 cards, King to 10, Ace to 11); then one face-up card decides; repeat until one player wins the pile.',
          gameplay: '1. Both players flip top card. 2. Higher wins; winner takes both to bottom. 3. Tie = War: lay cards face-down by rank (7â†’7 cards, Kâ†’10, Aâ†’11); flip last; higher wins all. 4. Repeat War if last cards tie. 5. Win all 52 cards.',
          keyRules: [
            "Simultaneous flip of top card from each player's stack.",
            'Higher rank wins the battle (Ace high); winner takes both cards to bottom of stack.',
            'Tie triggers war: escalation by rank (e.g. 7 lays 7 face-down, King 10, Ace 11), then one face-up; higher wins all.',
            'Repeat war if face-up cards tie; game ends when one player has all 52 cards.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Pure Chance. RÄƒzboi is a game of zero-volition; the deck order is fixed once dealt. However, in physical play, the way you place won cards at the bottom of your deck matters. Grouping face-cards together can lead to 'Streaks' that allow you to win several rounds in a row.",
          intermediate: "The 'End-Game' Shuffle: Physical cards often get 'clumped' after a large war. Since wars are often decided by face-cards, if you win a 20-card war, be careful how you order the won cards. Putting your high cards at the very bottom might mean you are vulnerable to a 'War' later when your stack is low.",
          advanced: "Stamina Management: The game can last a very long time. In competitive settings, 'Card-Tracking' isn't necessary, but mental focus is. If you lose several wars, stay calmâ€”the deck order will cycle back eventually.",
          tips: [
            "If you are playing with 3+ players, the person with the highest card takes everyone's card. If there's a tie, only the tying players go to war.",
            "The Ace is the most powerful card; protect it at all costs by hoping it doesn't get drawn into a war against another Ace.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
